{"term": "para mayores", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "content_warning", "concept": "explicit_content_label", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "euphemism", "crude": 5, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Phrase meaning 'for adults only,' used as a content warning for sexually explicit or age-restricted material online and in broadcast media.", "english_term": "for adults only / 18+", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard Spanish equivalent of NSFW or the 18+ label; used on websites, apps, and television alike. Non-transgressive in tone; purely regulatory."}
{"term": "sexo virtual", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "cybersex", "concept": "sexting", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 45, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual activity conducted via internet-based channels — live video calls, text chat, or audio — where participants engage sexually in real time without physical contact.", "english_term": "cybersex", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Broader than 'sexting,' which the registry maps to text/image exchange; sexo virtual encompasses live webcam sex, erotic video calls, and sexual roleplay chat. 'Sexting' is the closest registry concept but does not capture live interactive virtual sex."}
{"term": "stalkear", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "online_surveillance", "concept": "post_rejection_orbiting", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To obsessively monitor someone's social media presence and activity, typically in a romantic or sexual context, often without the target's knowledge.", "english_term": "to stalk (online)", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Direct borrowing of English 'stalk' with the Spanish productive -ear infinitive suffix. Broader than post_rejection_orbiting: stalkear can precede any romantic contact and implies active surveillance rather than passive engagement after rejection. Well-understood across all age-connected demographics."}
{"term": "ghosting", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "breakup_behavior", "concept": "ghosting", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 5, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The act of abruptly cutting off all communication with a romantic or sexual partner without explanation; borrowed directly from English.", "english_term": "ghosting", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Used without phonological or morphological adaptation across all Spanish-speaking regions. Recognized even by speakers who do not use it actively."}
{"term": "match sexual", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "dating_app_culture", "concept": "hookup", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 30, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A mutual match on a dating app — primarily Tinder — where both parties signal explicitly sexual rather than romantic intent; also the person so matched.", "english_term": "sexual match / hookup match", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Blends the English loanword 'match' (from app-dating culture) with 'sexual' to distinguish a hookup-oriented pairing from a romantic one. The concept precedes the encounter itself but presupposes it."}
{"term": "sugar daddy", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "transactional_relationship", "concept": "sugar_daddy", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 25, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "An older, typically wealthy man who provides money, gifts, or lifestyle support to a younger partner in exchange for companionship and sexual access; borrowed directly from English.", "english_term": "sugar daddy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Used without modification in Spanish. Circulates primarily in digital and social media contexts; well-understood across Spanish-speaking regions."}
{"term": "sugar mommy", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "transactional_relationship", "concept": "sugar_daddy", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 25, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "An older, typically wealthy woman who provides money or gifts to a younger partner in exchange for companionship and sexual access; the gender-inverted equivalent of 'sugar daddy.'", "english_term": "sugar mommy / sugar mama", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Registry contains only the male-coded 'sugar_daddy' concept; this entry is its gender-inverted counterpart. Slightly less common in Spanish than 'sugar daddy.' Also encountered as 'sugar mama' in some regions."}
{"term": "morbo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "transgressive_desire", "concept": "erotic_transgression", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 40, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 98, "recognition_pct_peak": 98, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "A distinctly Castilian concept denoting the erotic thrill of transgression: the dark, morbidly exciting arousal that arises from what is forbidden, taboo, or culturally off-limits, combining sexual excitement with a frisson of wrongness.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "No English concept captures the specific fusion of morbid fascination with erotic excitement. 'Titillation,' 'prurience,' and 'forbidden desire' each cover partial aspects but miss the keynote of pleasurable wrongness. Most strongly associated with Iberian Spanish; present but less culturally central in Latin American varieties. The word shares etymology with 'morbid' (Latin morbus, disease)."}
{"term": "ser un morbo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "transgressive_desire", "concept": "erotic_transgression", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 35, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "Predicative phrase meaning 'to be a source of transgressive erotic excitement,' applied to a person, situation, or object that produces the characteristic morbo thrill.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "'Ese tío es un morbo' = 'That guy is such a turn-on in a transgressive way.' Lexically, morbo is used here as a countable noun; the phrase foregrounds the quality as residing in the stimulus rather than the experiencer."}
{"term": "tener morbo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "transgressive_desire", "concept": "erotic_transgression", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 35, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "Verbal phrase meaning 'to possess the quality of transgressive erotic appeal,' used of a person or situation that inherently carries morbo.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "'Esa chica tiene mucho morbo' = 'That girl has a lot of transgressive erotic charge.' The possession framing emphasizes morbo as a stable attribute of the stimulus rather than a fleeting reaction."}
{"term": "dar morbo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "transgressive_desire", "concept": "erotic_transgression", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 40, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "Verbal phrase meaning 'to provoke or cause transgressive erotic excitement in another person,' emphasizing the directional transfer of the morbo sensation from stimulus to experiencer.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "'Me da morbo' = 'It/he/she gives me that transgressive erotic thrill.' The dar (give) construction is the most common of the morbo verbal collocations and the primary way the concept is expressed in conversation."}
{"term": "morbazo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "transgressive_desire", "concept": "erotic_transgression", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 55, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "Augmentative intensification of morbo; an overwhelming, intense jolt of transgressive erotic excitement, used when the sensation is especially strong or surprising.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The Spanish -azo augmentative suffix intensifies morbo to convey a sudden or exceptionally powerful surge of transgressive arousal. Less frequent than the base form; mainly used for emphatic effect in speech. 'Menudo morbazo' = 'What an intense burst of that dark erotic charge.'"}
{"term": "honra", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_honor", "concept": "sexual_honor_chastity", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "archaic", "crude": 0, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 96, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman's sexual honor or reputation for chastity; in classical and early modern Spanish culture, the social standing of a woman (or her male guardian) derived from her sexual purity and strict conformity to female modesty codes.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "English 'honor' or 'chastity' are partial translations; neither captures the specifically social, gendered, and transactable nature of honra as a commodity that can be lost, defended, or violently restored. Central to Golden Age Spanish drama (Calderón, Lope de Vega). Understood but largely archaic in modern Spanish; survives in fixed phrases and historical registers."}
{"term": "deshonra", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_dishonor", "concept": "sexual_dishonor", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual dishonor; the irreversible stain on a woman's or family's social standing resulting from premarital or extramarital sexual activity, framed as a public and permanent loss.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "English 'dishonor' lacks the same specific sexual and gendered charge; deshonra in traditional Spanish and Latin American societies was effectively a social death sentence, and its narrative restoration through marriage or violence drove entire dramatic genres. More archaic than honra but still culturally legible."}
{"term": "deshonrar", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_dishonor", "concept": "defloration", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To dishonor a woman sexually; to take her virginity or engage in illicit sexual relations that destroy her social reputation, whether through seduction, consent outside marriage, or force.", "english_term": "to deflower / to dishonor", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Maps approximately to 'defloration' but the emphasis is on the social consequence (loss of honor) rather than the physical act alone. Uniquely, the transgression is framed as the man's violation of social norms — rape, seduction, and consensual premarital sex are linguistically equivalent under this framework."}
{"term": "vergüenza", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_shame", "concept": "sexual_repression", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 5, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Shame or embarrassment related to sexual matters; in the honor/shame economy, the internalized social emotion that enforces sexual propriety by making transgression feel disgraceful and publicly visible.", "english_term": "shame / sexual shame", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "General word for shame/embarrassment; in sexual contexts it is the psychological enforcement mechanism of the honor economy. English 'shame' is approximate; it lacks the specifically social and family-honor dimension of vergüenza in traditional contexts, where one's sexual shame is also the family's."}
{"term": "pudor", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_modesty", "concept": "prudishness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 0, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual modesty, decency, and shame; the sense of bodily and moral propriety that leads one to conceal or avoid explicit discussion of sexual matters, often framed as a virtue in traditional and religious contexts.", "english_term": "modesty / prudishness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "More positive and classical in tone than English 'prudishness,' which implies excessive inhibition; pudor can describe either virtuous modesty or dysfunctional inhibition depending on context. From Latin pudor (shame, modesty); used in formal, legal, and literary registers. Atentado al pudor = indecent assault (legal)."}
{"term": "recato", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_modesty", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 0, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Circumspection, modesty, and sexual reserve as a social and moral virtue, particularly expected of women; the quality of keeping sexual matters private and behaving with proper restraint.", "english_term": "modesty / sexual reserve / decorum", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Recato is traditionally framed as praiseworthy, not repressive, distinguishing it from English 'prudishness.' Primarily describes female behavior; a woman described as 'de recato' is virtuous in the traditional frame, not inhibited. More archaic in everyday usage; survives in fixed phrases and religious discourse."}
{"term": "recatada", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_modesty", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 0, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Feminine adjective describing a woman as modest, sexually reserved, and circumspect; a woman who adheres to traditional codes of female sexual propriety.", "english_term": "modest / demure / sexually reserved", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Adjectival form of recato applied specifically to women. In traditional Spanish culture an unambiguous compliment; in contemporary usage it can carry ironic or condescending overtones when wielded by younger speakers to describe conservative female behavior."}
{"term": "indecente", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_impropriety", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 35, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 97, "recognition_pct_peak": 97, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Indecent; sexually offensive, improper, or violating norms of public decency, applied to behavior, language, or material.", "english_term": "indecent", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'indecent'; used in both legal registers (acto indecente, propuesta indecente) and colloquial condemnation. Functions across the full spectrum from mild prudishness to serious legal sanction."}
{"term": "indecencia", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_impropriety", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 35, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Indecency; the quality of being sexually improper or offensive, or a specific act that violates standards of sexual propriety.", "english_term": "indecency", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'indecency'; functions in colloquial, journalistic, and legal contexts. Often used in exclamation: '¡Qué indecencia!' expressing moral outrage at sexual impropriety."}
{"term": "obsceno/a", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_impropriety", "concept": "obscenity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 45, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 97, "recognition_pct_peak": 97, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Obscene; sexually explicit, offensive, or grossly indecent in a manner that violates community standards of public decency.", "english_term": "obscene", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'obscene'; the /a ending indicates the feminine adjectival form. Used in legal contexts (publicación obscena), moral condemnation, and colloquial speech. Slightly stronger register than 'indecente.'"}
{"term": "obscenidad", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_impropriety", "concept": "obscenity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 45, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Obscenity; sexually explicit or grossly offensive material or behavior that violates standards of public decency, or a specific instance thereof.", "english_term": "obscenity", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct cognate of English 'obscenity'; operates in legal definitions (obscenidad pública) and colloquial moral outrage alike. Countable ('una obscenidad') or uncountable ('la obscenidad')."}
{"term": "liviandad", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_looseness", "concept": "licentiousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 25, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": "LATAM", "definition": "Sexual looseness or moral frivolity; the quality of being lax, permissive, or irresponsible in sexual matters, implying a dangerous absence of moral gravity.", "english_term": "licentiousness / moral looseness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From liviano (light, insubstantial); the metaphor frames sexual morality as having weight — liviandad is the failure to take it seriously. More alive in Latin American Spanish than Iberian; somewhat archaic overall. Less severe than libertinaje; the slight softness of the term makes it useful for coded condemnation."}
{"term": "liviana", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_looseness", "concept": "wanton_behavior", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 40, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": "LATAM", "definition": "A sexually loose woman; a woman considered to have light morals or to be sexually promiscuous, applied pejoratively within the honor/shame framework.", "english_term": "loose woman / woman of easy virtue", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Substantivized or predicate-adjective use of the feminine form of liviano: 'es una liviana' or 'mujer liviana.' Functions as a mild-to-moderate pejorative in Latin American Spanish; less severe than 'puta' but occupying the same honor/shame vocabulary. Primarily LATAM; Iberian speakers would more likely use 'fresca' or 'ligera de cascos' for similar meaning."}
